dsh: reasoning:
Now I need to decide. The brief gives the COO's picks; I need to give my own picks with reasons based on the vision and use cases, citing my items.

Key constraints: "Keep to the vision and its use cases. A requirement must not mislead, must not be split, and holds no invented numbers." And "say what it costs" per recommendation.

Let me think each decision through with Sun Tzu's rules.

Decision 1: Does a known link marked with `dynamic` limit count as missing under A2?
Options a: No when a limit at its site names why (范蠡's wording). b: Yes.
COO pick a.
My prior position: question 3 in round 2, conditional; I kept the missing-link line without the conditional, but asked EJ. Under 老子's c, a dynamic link isn't "known" so no miss. The COO says c settles decision 1. But if decision 3 is c, decision 1's wording should probably be a or not? Under c, a dynamic link is not known, so "a known link missing" won't fire; no need for the "with no limit at its site naming why" conditional. But the owner asks decision 1 separately anyway. If we pick a's line wording, it's compatible with both c and b definitions: it's a narrower missing-link line. Actually a adds "with no limit at its site naming why"; under c, this is redundant but harmless? Hmm, a requirement shouldn't be split or mislead. Redundancy is duplication of L1's rule; my round 2 position rejected duplicating L1 (on decision 4/A5's limit line). But here, the issue is different: A2's missing-link line needs to not fire on a link that is marked dynamic. Adding the qualifier carves out the dynamic case explicitly. Under c (link known only when type checker establishes it), a dynamic-target link would be... hmm, wait: can the type checker establish a link to a dynamic target? For a dynamic call, the type checker might establish that a call expression exists and maybe some target; but the tool can't follow. If the type checker establishes the declaration but not implementation, is the link known? If a known link is "type checker establishes it" — for a member call on a service interface, the type checker establishes the interface member. The link known then. A dynamic limit at site names why it couldn't follow further. Under a, not missing.

Which Sun Tzu rule? #17: probe and read the signs; the limit is a sign, and a sign that names why is evidence. #11: foreknowledge must come from people/evidence; a dynamic limit is a stated limit, not silence. #8: speed, don't overbuild. #5: move only on advantage — the marginal gain of failing on a known-but-dynamic link is not a use case; the cost is a false "missing" / more work. #2: do not rely on the enemy not coming; rely on having the means — i.e., do not rely on dynamic links being absent; mark them and say why. Actually #2 argues for showing the limit at the site, which the tool does.

Also L1 says every branch that stops early names why; A2's missing-link line should fail only silent omissions. So a.

But what about the interplay with decision 3? If c defines link known as type-checker-established, then a's conditional never needs to fire; but the COO says c settles decision 1 by itself. There's tension: if c, then a is perhaps unnecessary. But the brief asks both, and owners may overrule later; my picks should be coherent. If I pick 3c and 1a, is that coherent? Under 3c, a dynamic-target link isn't "known" (type checker doesn't establish the target implementation), so it cannot count as missing. a's wording adds a redundant condition but doesn't contradict. Hmm, but a's line "a known link within the depth is missing with no limit at its site naming why" — under c, any known link is type-checker-established, and if marked dynamic at its site, not missing. That's consistent.

Actually the COO's claim "c settles decision 1 by itself" suggests with 3c, decision 1 should be a (not missing). But careful: c wording says "a link is known when the type checker establishes it". For a dynamic call, the type checker may well establish the call's existence/target declaration but not the runtime target. Is the *link* known? The link is from call site to target. If the target is dynamic, type checker can't establish it. So not known, hence no miss. So a. Good.

However, my previous round-2 self preferred 3b ("established from repository source or type information") over c, because c gave "two owners". Now the COO picks c. Do I hold 3b or accept c? The question is which serves the vision better. Let me reason.

A2's use cases: "Given code or a description, the tool locates the place" — place finding uses code/description, not type checker. A link is a call relation; the type checker establishes it. 老子's c splits the definition by object, which is precise and maps to the two commands. 3b's "repository source or type information" covers both but is vague: a link could be "established from repository source" by a regex, which would make the tool's own method the judge (范蠡's objection). 3b also doesn't settle the dynamic case (Linus says dynamic label can't excuse omission of a source-established link). Under 3b, if source shows a call expression, is the link known even if target is dynamic? Ambiguous. So c is sharper and settles decisions 1 and 2? Decision 2 is about a body written into a call — whether entering it adds a level. 老子's c doesn't settle that; no definition of level. Hmm.

Sun Tzu #16: orders clear first — the definition must be clear so it can be enforced. #4: know other and self; knowing is specific to the object and the evidence: a place is known from code/description (探), a link from type information (the tool's map is type-based per R2). #17: probe (策/作/形/角) — each probe learns a different thing; so a two-part definition is not a split but a distinction by what is learned. #1 chain: 地生度, 度生量... each step has its own measure.

But my round-2 self argued a word must have one owner; c has two clauses but each with its own owner. Is that "two owners for one word" a defect? The word "known" applies to two kinds of thing (place, link), and each kind has its own evidence. That's arguably precise, not ambiguous. #4's five ways to know victory: each different. #17's four probes: each answers a different question. So c is consistent with my rules.

I think I can accept c, and note it's a change from my round-2 wording (b) because it settles the two questions 1 and 2 without a conditional — but does it settle 2? No, only 1. The COO says "because it settles decision 1 by itself." Fine.

But wait: under c, is a place found from a description "known when the code or description points to it"? What if a description matches many places? Then each is a candidate, and ambiguity is flagged. Fine.

Decision 2: Does going into a body written straight into a call add a level?
a. No. b. Yes. COO pick a.
My prior position: round 2 Linus: "No extra chain level follows merely from lexical nesting. Each level requires a call link; ownership alone does not establish a callback invocation." I also argued in round 2 for depth = shortest route with level 0 and each call link adds one level. This strongly implies a: the body is entered without a call link; it's the same call expression. Under A2 "each link adds one level" and a link is a call/new/service-call. Entering a body via Effect.gen is not a call link from the surrounding function; the body is the argument. The spec's ChainStep: each level is a Link. So a is consistent with the spec and my earlier wording. Sun Tzu rules: #1 chain each step is a distinct measure (地→度→量...) — no link, no level; #14 numbers not valued for their own sake (also count the real depth); #16 clear orders (a level must have a unit); #4 know the ground (the default six must reach real calls; cost is not measured, so don't invent a level). Also #8 speed: a makes default 6 reach further, faster; b halves reach (COO's reading, not measured) and costs more queries. So a.

Cost: under a, a body that is a callback invoked later might be conflated? No—ownership still names the body. The risk: a body with no holder is placed at its lexical site, which may not reflect runtime call order. My #2: know what you don't know; L1/limit covers dynamic. Say cost: a body's own calls are still linked at the same level as the call that contains it, so a chain down may show a callback's callees at the same level as the callback's own siblings' callees? Hmm. Actually level counting: the place is level 0; each call link adds level. A call inside the body at the call site is one link from the place (the enclosing function? or the body?). A2's "place" can be the body itself (a place of its own). The chain through the body: from place to body has no call link, so body is level 0 too? That could mean two places at level 0. That's the cost of a: levels measure call links, not lexical nesting, so a body and its surrounding function are both level 0 for the chain. Need to state risk: a caller of the surrounding function is one level up; a caller of the body is also one level up, even though the body sits inside the surrounding function. The chain may place two distinct places at the same level, so "level" no longer orders lexical depth. But the requirement counts call distance, which is what matters for call chains. Cost: a reader expecting lexical nesting as levels will misread. That's a real risk to name.

Decision 3: "Known" in A2. options a no definition (范蠡), b source-or-type (Linus, me), c place=code/description, link=type checker (老子). COO pick c.
I lean c for the reasons above. Is there a Sun Tzu rule against defining? #16 orders clear; #1 count before commit — a definition is the count; #4 know other/self; #11 foreknowledge from evidence. #12? "seek it in 勢... " no. #14 "more is not valued" — don't over-define? c is two clauses, precise.

But my previous self specifically held against 老子's c because two owners for one word. Would changing now be inconsistent? The round-2 brief asked for final decision; now the COO asks again with a specific pick and says c settles decision 1. It's legitimate to change if the argument is better. But I should be honest: I can say c's two-part definition matches the two things being known, and it removes the conditional that b leaves for dynamic links. My round-2 objection was that the word had no single owner; on re-reading, each object has one owner, and that is the precision needed. Cite my #4 and #17 (each way of knowing is specific), and my #16 (the order must be clear). Hmm, but "my #4" is "know whether you can fight; know the other side and yourself" — the five ways to know victory are different. Good.

Alternatively, I could pick b and hold my previous position, but then decision 1 becomes a conditional (a's wording). The COO picked c and the brief says owner may overrule later. The task is to give my pick, not to agree. Which is better under the vision?

Vision: "It is written in TypeScript because opencode's calls go through typed, injected services (Effect's `yield* Permission.Service`), which a syntax-only reader cannot follow." So links are established by the type checker; places are found by code/description (`find`). c mirrors the two commands in the spec exactly: `find` locates places by code/description; calls are linked by the type checker's definition/implementation lookup (spec Call links table). Therefore c is grounded in the spec and vision. b's "repository source or type information" could let a link be established by grep, which R2 forbids (a declaration is never `to`; implementation lookup). Actually R2 says links are type-checker-based. So b is wider than R2 and could mislead. c is the right pick. I'll take c and say the change is because R2's table and the `find` command map one-to-one onto c; my round-2 b was too wide for links.

Wait, but there is a subtlety: c says "a place is known when the code or description points to it" — for `chain <file:line>`, a place is given by location, so code points to it. For `where <name>`, a name matches definitions. For `find "<code or words>"`, code text or description points to places. Yes.

Decision 4: Does A5 add "a limit the interface requires for that command is part of the answer"?
a. Yes (Linus, 老子). b. No; "its limits" already covers it (范蠡, me). COO pick b.
My prior: I rejected 6 in round 2 (proposal 6 = stop-short sentence + interface limit); I said L1 owns stops, and "A5's 'with its evidence and its limits' already covers it"? Actually my round 2 step 11 says "A5 keeps the owner's 'with its evidence and its limits' and leans on L1 for a stop". I rejected the interface-limit clause (proposal 6 as a whole), while 老子/Linus kept the interface-limit half. Now decision 4 asks exactly that. Should I stay with b?

Under b, "its limits" — A5 today says "A specific question gets that answer with its evidence and limits". "its limits" refers to the answer's limits, which include the interface-required limit (`{reason:"other", text:"no test-file pattern given"}`). So yes, it already covers it. Adding it duplicates. #1 take away? (That's Laozi's). Sun Tzu: #16 clear orders — does "its limits" unambiguously cover an interface-required limit? A limit is a limit; the spec's `Limit` type includes reason "other". So yes. #5 move only on advantage: adding a clause costs words and creates a second place to disagree, with no use case gained. #6 mix harm into benefit: the cost is ambiguity if "its limits" is read narrowly. Hmm.

The strongest argument for a: the `Limit` type's "other" reason may not be seen as a "stop-early" limit; L1 covers stops; an interface-required limit like "no test-file pattern given" is not a stop, so if A5 doesn't say it, nothing charges it. But A5 already says "with its evidence and limits" — "its limits" is not restricted to stops. So b is enough. A tool could omit the "no test pattern" limit and fail A5's "without ... its limits". So b holds.

COO picks b. I'll pick b, cite #16 (the order is already clear) and #5 (no gain from a second sentence), and #1? My #1 is reckoning; not apt. #3 whole over broken — don't split the answer's limit clause. Maybe #6: the harm of the added clause is a second owner for the same duty, two places to disagree. My round 2 step on A5 did exactly that with proposal 6. Good.

Decision 5: Layout of A2's and A5's Fails-if lines.
a. One line naming both actors (范蠡). b. A tool line and an agent line (Linus, 老子, me). COO pick b.
My prior: round 2 I chose actor-labelled lines, two bullets. So b, consistent. Reasons: #16 orders clear; each actor charged with its own duty; #4? "知彼知己" — the check must name who can fail. #12 regular/irregular? no. Use #16 and #1 (a check that mixes two actors cannot be counted: "多算勝" — count each line's duty separately). Also #13? "not every road is taken" — no.

Costs: new line strings mean case mapping; two lines for one requirement; A2/A5 partly NOT CHECKED until P6. Name that.

Decision 6: A3's fake rule on opencode. Does a test that swaps in a fake service reach the real function?
a. exclude: no. The test reaches the fake → left out.
b. count: yes counted.
c. report both: count without fakes + fake-path tests listed separately.
COO pick a (Linus and 老子 in P1).
Sun Tzu rules: #4 know other and self — the test reaches the fake, not the real; knowing the distinction. #17 probe and read signs — the path through the fake is a sign of a test of the fake, not the real. #1 count before commit — count the real reach, not a false one. #3 whole over broken? Prefer a whole truth to a broken count? Hmm. #14 numbers not valued for their own sake — a count of fake paths would inflate the count. #2 make yourself unbeatable — no false claims. #11 foreknowledge from evidence — the evidence is the static path through the fake, which does not establish reaching the real function. So a.

But c preserves information: report both. Under a, the fake-path tests are left out entirely — the user doesn't see them. A3 fails if "a test with a known path is missing". Does a test that swaps in a fake have a "known path" to the real function? The static chain goes test → fake, not to real. So no. But c would be safer for not hiding: list fake-path tests separately so they're not counted but are visible. The COO's pick a. Which is better under Sun Tzu? #6 mix harm into benefit: excluding loses information (harm), but the user's fixed rule is exclude, and A3 says the user sets the rule. The point of the run is to set the value on opencode. a is simpler, count matches list, no confusion. c adds a third category not in the schema? The spec's TestsAnswer has `tests` and `undecided`; c would require a new field or use undecided. `--fake-rule count|exclude` only has two values; c is a new third behavior, not in the spec. So picking c would require changing the interface. a is within the spec. Sun Tzu #16: the rule is set; #13? not every command accepted; #14 numbers not valued for their own sake. #3 "whole over broken": report both keeps the answer whole? Hmm, "whole" in #3 means preserve, don't break/destroy. Excluding fake-path tests destroys information. But the user's rule explicitly says whether a fake reaches; exclude means those tests don't reach the real function, so omitting them from "tests that reach the function" is truthful. The user can still ask `tests` with `--fake-rule count` or look at undecided. Under a, they're "left out" of tests and undecided per spec. That's the user's rule. So a.

Cost: a hides tests that exercise a fake path; a reader may think no test touches this area. But A3's scope is "tests that reach the function through the code", and a fake test doesn't. L1? Not a stop. The cost is a lost signal; c would preserve it. Mention it. Also #6 harm: excluding fakes loses the fake-path tests; the harm is acceptable because the count stays true, and `undecided` already exists for the unset rule.

Hmm, should I propose c as "proposed change to the pattern"? No; the question is a decision. Could note c as a later option but not pick.

Decision 7: A1's sibling rule on opencode. Which files count as siblings?
a. files under one folder implementing same declared interface or service tag (Dave in P1).
b. files exporting same member names, shown as candidates (老子 in P1).
c. one glob per group, given by user per module: e.g. `tool/*.ts` minus registry.
COO pick c, with a's rule used to choose globs for the three opencode modules.
Sun Tzu: #17 learn by probing and signs — use the interface/tag to choose the glob. #16 orders clear — the user's `--siblings <glob>` is the interface; the spec already takes it with no default. #2 make yourself unbeatable: the user sets the sibling groups, so the tool cannot be wrong? #1 reckon before committing: c requires the owner to do the reckoning per module (choose globs) before the run; a is automatic but may be wrong. #4 know other and self — choose by declared interface. #13 not every road taken. #14 numbers not valued. 

Which is better for the vision? A1 says "Which files are siblings is decided by a fixed rule the user sets." c is literally the user's glob, fixed per module. a is a fixed rule based on interface/tag, automatic. b is candidates. The COO picks c with a's rule to choose the globs. Under Sun Tzu, #4 "know the ground" — the interface/tag is the ground to choose the glob from; the tool's job is the map. c keeps the tool's rule the user's, matching A1's text. Cost: the user must choose globs per module; a wrong glob hides siblings silently (no limit? spec says with no --siblings, there's a limit; with a wrong glob, no limit). The glob rule misses files that share an interface but live in different folders. a would find them automatically but can over-include. c is explicit and checkable. Pick c.

But note my #3 whole over broken: c risks a broken sibling table if the glob is wrong; but the user owns the rule. #2: do not rely on the enemy not coming — don't rely on the tool guessing; rely on the user's stated glob. #16: orders clear. So c.

Decision 8: What counts as a test file?
a. folder `test/`/`__tests__` or `*.test.ts`/`*.spec.ts`.
